    "id": "news-1788130881534-1",
    "title": "Somalia and Turkey Mark 104th Anniversary of Victory Day in Mogadishu",
    "original_title": "Soomaaliya Iyo Türkiye Oo Muqdisho Ku Xusay Maalinta Guusha",
    "slug": "somalia-and-turkey-mark-104th-anniversary-of-victory-day-in-mogadishu",
    "summary": "Somali federal ministers joined Turkish diplomats in Mogadishu on August 30 to commemorate Turkey's Victory Day and Armed Forces Day, marking the 104th anniversary of the 1922 battle. The event highlighted deepening bilateral ties between the two nations.",
    "content": "Somali government officials and representatives of the Turkish embassy in Mogadishu gathered on August 30 to observe the 104th anniversary of Turkey's Victory Day, a national holiday commemorating the decisive Battle of Dumlupınar in 1922. The ceremony, organized by the Turkish Embassy, took place in the Somali capital and drew senior figures from both sides.\n\nAmong those in attendance were Somalia's Minister of Interior, Federal and Reconciliation Affairs Ali Yusuf Ali, also known as Ali Xoosh, as well as the Minister of Finance Bihi Iman Cige, Minister of Fisheries and Blue Economy Ahmed Hassan Adan, and Minister of Religious Affairs Mukhtar Robow Ali. Their presence underscored the significance that Somalia places on its relationship with Turkey.\n\nThe annual celebration marks a key moment in Turkish history when Turkish forces secured a decisive victory that helped shape the modern Turkish state. This year's event in Mogadishu was particularly notable as it highlighted the expanding cooperation between Ankara and Mogadishu in areas such as defense, infrastructure, and economic development.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this commemoration serves as a reminder of Turkey's active role in Somalia's reconstruction efforts. Turkey has been one of the most visible international partners in Somalia, operating schools, hospitals, and infrastructure projects, and providing military training. The presence of high-level Somali ministers at the event signals continuity in this strategic partnership, which is often seen as a model of South-South cooperation.\n\nAs Somalia continues to stabilize and rebuild, its ties with Turkey are likely to remain a key pillar of its foreign relations. The celebration of Turkey's national day in Mogadishu, attended by senior Somali officials, reflects a mutual commitment that has tangible benefits for both nations, and contributes to the broader security and development goals that also matter to the diaspora community.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/30/104-sano-kadib-turkiye-oo-guushii-taariikhiga-ahayd-lagu-xusay-muqdisho/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=104-sano-kadib-turkiye-oo-guushii-taariikhiga-ahayd-lagu-xusay-muqdisho",
